    {
      "key": "content:demo-content-reversible-mistakes",
      "kind": "content",
      "id": "demo-content-reversible-mistakes",
      "updatedAt": "2026-05-31T17:00:00.000Z",
      "data": {
        "type": "blog",
        "slug": "reversible-mistakes",
        "title": "A Small Atlas of Reversible Mistakes",
        "summary": "A field guide to experiments that remain useful even when the first hypothesis is wrong.",
        "body": "# A Small Atlas of Reversible Mistakes\n\nA reversible mistake leaves the workspace more legible than it found it. It has a named input, a bounded action, and an observation that survives the failed hypothesis. The opposite is not failure; it is an experiment that changes several hidden variables and gives the next reader no path home.\n\n## Preserve the return path\n\nBefore changing a stateful system, record the version and the smallest stable identity of the data. Then prefer a transaction that either publishes every related record or none of them. The useful invariant is simple: `ready` must never describe a partial dataset.\n\n```js\nawait repository.transaction(stores, 'readwrite', async (tx) => {\n  await replaceFixtureRecords(tx, seed);\n  await tx.put('meta', readyMetadata);\n});\n```\n\nThe code is less important than the order encoded by it. Readers can now ask whether an interrupted write was committed, whether the checksum changed, and whether reset returns the same graph. Those questions are portable across engines.\n\n## Make uncertainty visible\n\nA blocked database upgrade is not a generic startup failure. It means another living document still owns a connection. A quota error is not corrupt data. It means a complete transaction could not be committed within the browser's current storage policy. Naming those states gives the interface an honest recovery action instead of an opaque retry loop.\n\nThe atlas stays small on purpose. Each entry should fit on one screen: observed state, controlled change, result, and next branch. Over time these cards become a shared language for debugging without becoming a diary of every console line.",
        "authorId": "demo-user-north-window",
        "status": "published",
        "tags": ["demo-tag-reproducibility", "demo-tag-debugging"],
        "locale": "en",
        "format": "markdown",
        "coverAssetKey": null,
        "attachmentKeys": [],
        "createdAt": "2026-05-26T13:00:00.000Z",
        "publishedAt": "2026-05-31T16:45:00.000Z",
        "sortOrder": 20
      }
